       "Triploblastic animals have three germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm) — from Platyhelminthes to Chordata.",
       "Acoelomate — Platyhelminthes; pseudocoelomate — Aschelminthes; coelomate — Annelida to Chordata.",
       "Phylum Porifera (sponges) shows a water canal system and collar cells (choanocytes) — e.g., Sycon, Spongilla, Euplectella.",
       "Phylum Coelenterata (Cnidaria) has stinging cells (cnidoblasts) and two forms — polyp and medusa; e.g., Hydra, Aurelia, Adamsia.",
       "Phylum Ctenophora bears comb plates — called sea walnuts; e.g., Ctenoplana, Pleurobrachia.",
       "Phylum Platyhelminthes (flatworms) excrete through flame cells — e.g., Taenia (tapeworm), Fasciola (liver fluke).",
       "Phylum Aschelminthes (roundworms) have a pseudocoelom — e.g., Ascaris, Wuchereria (filaria worm), Ancylostoma (hookworm).",
       "Phylum Annelida shows true segmentation and a closed circulatory system — e.g., earthworm (Pheretima), leech (Hirudinaria), Nereis.",
       "Phylum Arthropoda is the largest phylum — jointed appendages and chitinous exoskeleton; e.g., cockroach (Periplaneta), prawn, butterfly.",
